  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 251 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nToday through tonight...\n\nLatest satellite observations depict low clouds have mostly cleared \nout across central Indiana as northwest flow continues to filter in \ndrier air. Mostly clear skies along with winds becoming light from a \nweakening pressure gradient has allowed for temperatures to quickly \ndrop well into the 30s already. Expect temperatures to continue \ncooling with lows in the upper 20s to mid 30s. Patchy fog \ndevelopment also appears increasingly likely in climatologically \nfavored locations as winds have been very light or calm at times.\nAny fog that develops should quickly mix out after daybreak. \nScattered to broken lake enhanced low clouds still remain across \nfar northeastern counties and will likely persist through at least\nthe first half of the day. These clouds may limit diurnal \ntemperature swings to some degree. \n\nSurface high pressure centered near northern Missouri early this \nmorning will shift eastward through the day today promoting quiet \nweather conditions. Mostly clear skies and light winds tonight may \nonce again support patchy fog development in climatologically favored \nlocations, but winds will increase slightly late overnight leading \nto some uncertainty. Look for highs in the low to mid 50s today with \nlows dropping into the upper 20s to low 30s tonight.  \n\nSunday through late next next week...\n\nQuiet weather conditions are expected to continue through the middle \nof next week as surface high pressure generally remains in control. \nEnsemble guidance depicts a few subtle systems pushing weak cold \nfronts into the region, but gulf moisture remaining cutoff will keep \nthese fronts moisture starved, allowing for dry conditions to \npersist. A cold front will move through on Sunday bringing breezy \nconditions with southwesterly wind gusts between 20 to 30 mph \npossible. Winds should then quickly taper off into Sunday night once \nthe pressure gradient relaxes as the cold front departs. Light winds \ncombined with mostly clear skies could support additional fog \ndevelopment Sunday night into Monday morning.  \n\nA few shortwaves are expected to move through the region next week \nafter the cold front on Sunday passes. These upper waves will likely \npush through a few more fronts through the area during the week. The \nonly system that could potentially support precipitation is a \nshortwave moving in from the northwest late Wednesday into Thursday. \nWeak to modest forcing and very limited moisture return supports a \nlow (20-30%) chance for rain. \n\nReturn flow will warm highs into the 60s early next week. Strong \ncold air advection associated with deep troughing and a cold front\nis then likely going to cool temperatures significantly mid-late \nnext week. Highs are only expected to reach the low to mid 50s by\nThursday and highs will struggle to get out of the 40s on Friday.",
